This tutorial walks through writing a first CSLv3 specification. Readers need a recent build of the reference parser, a text editor that can save files as UTF-8, and approximately twenty minutes. Prior experience with a typed programming language helps but is not required. By the end the reader will have a working specification file that parses, type-checks, and pretty-prints cleanly.

The first step is to open the editor and create a new file called hello.csl. The first line declares a section. Section declarations in CSLv3 use the glyph named section-sign. The reader can type this glyph directly if the keyboard supports it, or use the ASCII alias which the parser accepts as equivalent. Everything inside a section is indented relative to the section header, and the section ends when the indentation level drops back below the header. Inside the first section, the reader adds a second-level section that declares a simple data type. The type declaration syntax will feel familiar to anyone who has written in an ML-family language, with a type name on the left, an equals sign, and a type expression on the right.

Once the file is written, the reader runs the parser against it from a shell. The parser prints either a clean parse confirmation or a list of diagnostics. For a first file, the diagnostics are usually about indentation mismatches, so the reader should read each diagnostic carefully and check the line number it references. If the file parses cleanly, the reader tries adding a pretty-print command to the parser invocation. The pretty-printer has several modes and the canonical mode is the one used most often. It reformats the file to the project-standard layout and preserves the meaning of the original exactly. Passing the file back through the pretty-printer should produce the same text the reader started with, barring trivial whitespace normalization, which is a useful sanity check.

The reader then extends the spec with a function declaration. Functions in CSLv3 are written with a name, a parameter list, a return type, and optionally a body. For this tutorial the body is left absent. The parser will still accept a function signature without a body, which lets the reader sketch an interface before committing to an implementation. Running the parser again should show the function in the pretty-printer output, formatted consistently with the rest of the file. If the function fails to parse, the reader should check that the arrow glyph is in the right position and that parameter types are separated by commas.

To explore diagnostics, the reader intentionally introduces an error by changing a type name to something that does not exist, or by removing the equals sign from a declaration. Running the parser with the strict flag and observing how the diagnostic changes shows that the strict flag promotes warnings to errors and causes the parser to exit with a non-zero status code, which is what continuous integration needs.

The reference corpus in the eval directory contains seven small specifications that exercise the full range of notation features. The reader is advised to read through them in order from C1 to C7. Each one is paired with an English translation in a neighboring file, which helps if any of the glyphs are unfamiliar. Once comfortable with the notation, the reader can look at the parser decisions log for the reasoning behind non-obvious design choices, and the specs directory for the formal grammar and tokenizer tables.

Notation is a tool and not a destination. Readers are encouraged to use it where it helps and write plain English where it does not, because the parser accepts both.
